Enable the SoC thermal sensor, so something throttles this device.

Nothing does today. /sys/class/thermal is empty, because our device tree turns
the sensor off:

	&thermal {
		status = "disabled";
	};

with no comment recording why. Everything else is already in place and unused:

  - mt8163.dtsi declares the sensor at 0x1100b000 (mediatek,mt8163-thermal)
    with its clocks, reset, auxadc and apmixedsys references;
  - the thermal_calibration nvmem cell exists in the efuse node at 0x10206000
    and the sensor references it;
  - thermal-zones already declares cpu-thermal, gpu-thermal and soc-thermal
    against that sensor, with trip points;
  - cpufreq is up, so the cooling devices have somewhere to bind;
  - the driver is in tree at drivers/thermal/mediatek/auxadc_thermal.c.

So this is a status change plus two config symbols.

MTK_THERMAL and MTK_SOC_THERMAL are both enabled explicitly rather than left to
defconfig, because MTK_SOC_THERMAL sits inside `if MTK_THERMAL` - a depends-on
rather than a select. scripts/config will happily set the inner symbol while
the outer one is missing and leave a driver that never builds, which is the same
trap that INPUT_MATRIXKMAP (r188) and LEDS_LP55XX_COMMON would have been.

The likely reason it was disabled during bring-up is the nvmem-cells dependency:
if the efuse read fails, the sensor probe fails with it. Worth knowing if this
regresses - the symptom would be a probe failure rather than the silent nothing
we have now.

This matters more now than it did. Wi-Fi is the next item, and this is a sealed
enclosure that already drives a speaker amplifier with no thermal feedback at
all.

